        "status": "not-admitted",
        "reason": "PARKED 2026-10-26, and the repository archived the same day. The practitioner: \"We should archive the widget-pack-sea-level-rise. We already have a working model for worked examples.\" ... \"The pack idea was never tested extensively. We may revisit it at some time in the future or not.\"\n\nSo this is not a withdrawal: `withdrawn` would record a decision against the idea, and the decision is weaker than that. `not-admitted` remains exactly true -- the three required reviews are still not-started and the pack never was admitted -- and the entry stays so that runs which recorded its digest remain traceable, which is the rule this catalogue already states for a pack that stops moving.\n\nWhat the worked-example model does instead, and why it won: an example is admitted at a commit with per-file digests and carries its own licence, which is the same trust root as a pack, without a pack's second job of shipping vocabulary and widgets. The three sea-level widget contracts were implemented in the HOST all along, and the vocabulary is vendored at ontology/packs/sea-level.ttl; so the pack was supplying a mechanism for something Fieldwork was already doing another way.\n\nNothing was deleted. The three slr_* widgets, the vendored vocabulary and shapes, and the Manila worked example all stay. Revisiting means reviving the reviews, not rebuilding the pack.\n\nEARLIER REASON, kept: Not reviewed. Pack 0.2.0 replaces the workflow key component with a declared basis plus basisKind, because the AR6 confidence output files are p-boxes over several workflows and carry no workflow dimension \u2014 measured 2026-10-09. The three widget contracts are implemented in the host, and this pack supplies the vocabulary, the shapes and the worked example, which the host vendors at these pinned digests. Admission turns on the three required reviews.",
