  "aggregate_points_to_h3_cell": {
   "version": "0.2.0",
   "released": "2026-10-19",
   "changes": [
    {
     "kind": "minor",
     "description": "Renamed from `hex_aggregate` to `aggregate_points_to_h3_cell`. The old identifier keeps resolving: a saved workflow naming `hex_aggregate` is rewritten on open and stamped with sourceMigration, because the operation is unchanged and a rename must not refuse work the way a retirement does. The verb came after its object and the areal unit was unstated, while its sibling said verb first. Both now read as one operation over a named unit: aggregate points to H3 cells. The unit is the ontology's own term (fw:HexCell, 'H3 cell'), not 'hexbin', because a plotting library's hexbin is a lattice fitted to its data and these cells come from a fixed global index. No contract change: ports, output, parameters and behaviour are identical."
    },
    {
     "kind": "patch",
     "description": "Retitled from \"Group points in H3 cells\". The node type names hexagonal aggregation and the title named the indexing method instead, so the two shared no word and a reader had to know that H3 cells are hexagons to connect them. Flagged by the naming ratchet added 2026-10-13 under title-type-agreement; the convention's own rule is that the node type and the title must say the same thing. No contract change: ports, output, parameters and behaviour are identical, which is why this is a patch."
    },
    {
     "kind": "initial",
     "description": "Bounded geoprivacy worked example; see docs/experiments/35-cholera-geoprivacy-workflow.md."
    }
