  "reachable_corridor": {
   "version": "0.3.0",
   "released": "2026-10-14",
   "changes": [
    {
     "kind": "initial",
     "description": "The second half of isochrone: walks each street leaving a reached junction as far as the budget left there allows, and buffers the result. Fifth of six primitives an isochrone composition needs, and the one that makes the chain produce a polygon. Restates nothing -- remainingM and travelDirection arrive on the junctions, so the corridor cannot disagree with the traversal that produced them, and junctions from two traversals are refused rather than mixed. Extracting it moved the edge-orientation check and the part-way truncation into src/network-edges.ts, which isochrone now imports too, so the 2 metre endpoint tolerance exists once rather than twice. Takes a UTM zone because buffering in degrees is wrong, which makes nine widgets that each ask for one. See docs/experiments/84-reachable-corridor.md."
    },
    {
     "kind": "added",
     "description": "A corridor drawn from banded junctions now carries that band's minutes on the polygon, names the feature by it, and says in its notes that a band's corridor contains every shorter band's. Junctions from more than one band in a single corridor are refused, since one polygon cannot stand for two travel times. The minutes attribute already travels into receipts as fw:travelTimeMinutes, which isochrone has always set. Additive: unbanded input behaves as before."
    },
    {
     "kind": "changed",
     "description": "The corridor is the whole route buffered, and the buffer is charged. The walk off the street at the end of a route is now min(bufferM, the budget left at that point), so the corridor is full width while there is time to spare and tapers to nothing where the time runs out, instead of claiming bufferM of ground beyond the budget all the way round its boundary. When the junctions carry the origin’s location, the straight first leg from the site is part of the corridor too. bufferM therefore means the furthest anyone is assumed to walk off a street, not a drawing width. Measured on the John Snow five-minute band: 186,035 m² to 160,415 m². Experiment 114."
    }
   ],
